  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 148 PM EDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nTuesday - Wednesday...\n\nThe pesky cutoff low will meander back to the NW on Tuesday, \nsettling somewhere near the GA/TN/NC borders. As this occurs, expect \nthe NW extent of the associated moisture plume to reach up into our \nSE CWA. This combined with diurnal heating should allow for some \nafternoon and evening showers and storms. The vertical shear profile \nwill be very light, so any storm modes will be pulse in nature and \nof the general garden variety. Overall instability values will also \nbe fairly modest for this time of year, likely peaking in the 700-\n1200 J/KG range. Another factor suggesting strong storms are not \nlikely.\n\nThe closed low finally begins to open up and will start to lift \nthrough the central Appalachians as an open wave Wednesday and \nWednesday night. This will occur as broad and low amplitude baggy \ntroughing settles into the Great Lakes region, and the slightly \nstronger westerlies associated with it finally pick up the \nmeandering upper low. By this time, the bulk of the moisture plume \nwill be along and south of the Ohio River, so expect this will be \nthe best placement for renewed shower and storm development on \nWednesday afternoon and evening. Much like Tuesday, only expect \ngarden variety showers and a few storms as vertical wind shear \nremains non-existent and only modest instability values are able to \ndevelop. Strong storms not likely again on this day. Temps during \nthis stretch will likely range in the mid 80s each day, with lows in \nthe mid 60s to around 70.\n\nThursday - Sunday...\n\nFairly active weather will likely continue late week into the \nweekend as we sit between the western periphery of an upper ridge \ncentered off the Mid-Atlantic coast, and the baggy trough mentioned \nabove. A decent mix of Gulf and Atlantic moisture will likely be \nable to feed into our area around the upper ridge. In addition, a \nweak frontal boundary will slide southward toward the Ohio River \nwith a bit of additional moisture, and the near-surface convergence \nnecessary to trigger some diurnally driven showers and storms. It \nappears that the actual surface front may never quite make it into \nour region, but will likely get close enough to bring repeated \nrounds of showers and storms each day, especially across southern \nIN. Additional scattered coverage of airmass thunderstorms is also \npossible across KY moreso from the Gulf/Atlantic deeper moisture \nfeed. Coverage would likely be less in KY, and higher up along the \nfront where better triggering. The northern storms will also have a \nbit more deep layer shear to work with thanks to the upper \ntroughing. Perhaps there will be enough shear at times to support \nsome loosely organized updrafts capable of multicell cluster storm \nmodes. It is possible that some rainfall totals will start to add up \nthrough the the weekend as the combination of a quasi-stationary \nfrontal boundary, a plentiful deep moisture feed, and relatively \nslow storm movement combine to create some locally heavy rainfall \npotential. Will monitor trends to see if any potential flooding \nthreats could develop. Highs should generally range in the 85 to 90 \ndegree range for most spots through each day, although afternoon \nhighs may be somewhat dependent on storm activity each day.",
